<p>Computes component biomass (kg per tree, Mg/ha per plot × period), carbon and CO₂ equivalent from your tree data. The site contains no species coefficients, wood densities, expansion factors, root ratios or carbon fractions; you enter all of them and their sources are written with the results.</p>
<p><b>What it does not do:</b> it does not estimate missing heights (the Height–Diameter tool does); it does not compute a species without a set with another species' equation; it does not assume the components add up; it cannot check that the volume definition matches the factors.</p>
<h3>Data and approach</h3>
<ul>
<li>Data: a <b>Tree table</b> (plot, diameter at breast height and plot size required; tree, species, height, period optional) or the active approved data package. The simulated table is for trying out only; it carries no coefficients.</li>
<li><b>Allometric equations</b>: enter the components (e.g. stem, branches, foliage) and the carbon fraction of each (CF, 0–1). In the <b>Species → coefficient set</b> table choose a set for every species and component; "Apply this column to every species" does it explicitly. If an optional separate total equation is chosen, its difference from the component sum is written. Sets come from the editor on the page or from the Coefficient Library; ln and log₁₀ forms are separate, and the Baskerville correction is not applied to a log₁₀ equation (source awaited).</li>
<li><b>Additive component system</b> ("Fit an additive component system from your own data" in Settings): from a table of your felled and weighed trees (diameter, height, component masses; total optional) the component equations and the total-tree equation are fitted together: Parresol (1999) restricted SUR. For each component choose the terms (D, D², D²H, H; the intercept is always present) and an optional weight (σ² ∝ X^k, k given or estimated from the residuals). The total equation holds every term of the components and its coefficients are restricted to the sums of the component coefficients, so the predictions are additive on every tree. The result shows restricted SUR and the separate fits side by side (coefficient, SE, Se, bias, FI) and the residual correlations between equations; the sets are saved to the coefficient library with a source description (covariance; for equal-variance equations the residual standard error).</li>
<li><b>Nonlinear additive system</b> (same card, "System: nonlinear, power forms"): the components are B = a·D^b, a·D^b·H^c or a·(D²H)^b; the total equation is the sum of the component functions with the same coefficients and the system is fitted by Parresol (2001) nonlinear SUR (NSUR). Two error structures: additive error with the weight ψ = exp(gᵀθ) (ψ = D^θ, H^θ, (D²H)^θ, (D·H)^θ, D^θ1·e^(θ2·H) or D^θ1·e^(θ2·H²); θ given or estimated from the residuals of the unweighted fit), or every equation fitted on the logarithmic scale. The result shows NSUR and the separate fits (procedure 1) side by side, σ̂²<sub>NSUR</sub>, the residual correlations and, for a tree you enter (D, H), the confidence interval for the mean and the prediction interval for a new tree of every equation (95%, Student t). Only the component sets are saved (as the registered power forms); the total is their sum in the mapping. In an unweighted system with the total taken as the sum of the components the total equation carries no new information and nothing is fitted: choose weights or use the logarithmic scale.</li>
<li><b>Conversion from volume</b>: volumes come from a column per tree (m³) or from the <b>volumes sent by the Stem Section Volume tool</b>. To send them, link the approved data in Stem Section Volume and use "Send the volumes" in the results; they are read only for the same data package (same fingerprint), taken unrounded, and the package is not changed. Needed: the bark side of the volume and of the factors (different sides compute nothing), D and BEF or BCEF, R (below-ground / above-ground), CF and the source of the factors (required). The volume definition must be the one the factors were defined for.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Results</h3>
<ul>
<li>With <b>Both</b>, the same trees are computed by both routes; each approach is in its own row and they are not averaged.</li>
<li>Species without a set, trees without height and trees without volume with their row numbers; the number of trees computed with an estimated height or outside the validity range.</li>
<li>Per plot × period: components, total, C and CO₂ (Mg/ha). A unit with a tree that could not be computed gets no total (–). Plot × period without records count as "not measured" or "empty".</li>
<li><b>Uncertainty (Monte Carlo)</b>, allometric approach: only the coefficient covariance matrix and the residual standard error given by the source of a set (the set's optional "Definition of the equation" fields) are simulated. Choose the number of replicates, the seed and the interval level; the same seed gives the same numbers. In each replicate the coefficients of a set are drawn once and shared by every tree using it; the residual is drawn separately for each tree. Results: per plot × period and per tree the point value, mean, SD, CV and percentile interval; what each set contributes is written. If no set gives any information, "no uncertainty information" is written and nothing is computed; sets without information are held at their point values and the interval is marked incomplete. No uncertainty is computed for the conversion from volume.</li>
<li>Downloads: Excel (Info sheet with the sources of the sets and factors, the volume source and the rules; Units, Trees; comparison sheets for both approaches), trees as CSV and the JSON run record.</li>
</ul>
